1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,161
The Private Company That
Ran Singapore's Harbour,

2
00:00:05,241 --> 00:00:06,525
1864 to 1905

3
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:11,035
In 1905 the government of the
Straits Settlements took over

4
00:00:11,115 --> 00:00:15,858
the Tanjong Pagar Dock Company
and paid its shareholders about

5
00:00:15,938 --> 00:00:20,136
$755 for every $100 share,
a price that was still being

6
00:00:20,216 --> 00:00:25,350
questioned in the British House
of Commons more than a year later.

7
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:29,347
The company had begun in 1864
as the first local joint-stock

8
00:00:29,427 --> 00:00:32,812
company to offer shares to
the public in Singapore,

9
00:00:32,892 --> 00:00:36,482
and by the time it was bought
out it controlled nearly

10
00:00:36,562 --> 00:00:40,151
all of the docks and wharves
at the deep-water harbour

11
00:00:40,231 --> 00:00:43,900
that had made Singapore a
coaling stop for steamships.

12
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:46,450
Steamships had to be fed.

13
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:50,202
When the Peninsular and Oriental
Steam Navigation Company

14
00:00:50,282 --> 00:00:53,765
(P&O) began monthly sailings
to the Far East in 1845,

15
00:00:53,845 --> 00:00:57,799
with a stop at Singapore, the
coal for its ships was carried

16
00:00:57,879 --> 00:01:01,765
out from ships anchored at the
mouth of the Singapore River

17
00:01:01,845 --> 00:01:05,665
on small lighters, stored in
godowns along the river bank,

18
00:01:05,745 --> 00:01:09,375
and then lightered out again
to each arriving steamer.

19
00:01:09,375 --> 00:01:14,236
It was slow work, worse in choppy
weather when a lighter could capsize,

20
00:01:14,316 --> 00:01:17,100
and damp stored coal caught fire easily.

21
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:22,066
In 1852 P&O became the first
shipping company to move its coaling

22
00:01:22,146 --> 00:01:27,425
station from the river to New
Harbour, where it built its own wharf.

23
00:01:27,425 --> 00:01:31,567
The anchorage was sheltered, the
water was deep, and there was

24
00:01:31,647 --> 00:01:35,800
room for coal stores and
warehouses, so other firms followed.

25
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:39,575
New Harbour was renamed
Keppel Harbour in 1900,

26
00:01:39,655 --> 00:01:45,152
after Admiral Henry Keppel, who had
found deep water so close to the

27
00:01:45,232 --> 00:01:49,825
shore in 1848 when he sailed
HMS Meander in for repairs.

28
00:01:49,825 --> 00:01:52,725
Ship repair was the next need.

29
00:01:52,725 --> 00:01:56,842
In 1859 a British mariner,
Captain William Cloughton,

30
00:01:56,922 --> 00:02:01,831
built Singapore's first dry dock
at New Harbour, and the Patent

31
00:02:01,911 --> 00:02:06,425
Slip and Dock Company was
formed in 1861 to take it over.

32
00:02:06,425 --> 00:02:09,903
In 1864 a group of investors decided to

33
00:02:09,983 --> 00:02:13,450
build another dry dock at New Harbour.

34
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:18,621
To raise the money they set up a
joint-stock limited liability company,

35
00:02:18,701 --> 00:02:22,467
the Tanjong Pagar Dock
Company, which was registered

36
00:02:22,547 --> 00:02:26,387
on the 29th of September
1864 and was the first local

37
00:02:26,467 --> 00:02:31,200
company of that kind to offer
shares to the public in Singapore.

38
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,558
The agency house Guthrie
& Co. and the Chinese

39
00:02:34,638 --> 00:02:38,150
merchant Tan Kim Ching
were among its founders.

40
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:42,111
The company hoped to raise
$200,000 in Singapore,

41
00:02:42,191 --> 00:02:47,554
in 2,000 shares of $100 each, but
not all of the shares were taken

42
00:02:47,634 --> 00:02:52,500
up locally and the balance was
sold to investors in London.

43
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,566
Its articles of association
gave a London Consulting

44
00:02:56,646 --> 00:03:00,154
Committee a veto over
spending above $20,000,

45
00:03:00,234 --> 00:03:04,300
an arrangement that would
matter forty years later.

46
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:08,918
Tan Kim Ching was the eldest of
the three sons of Tan Tock Seng,

47
00:03:08,998 --> 00:03:12,735
and after his father's death
he ran the family firm,

48
00:03:12,815 --> 00:03:18,100
which became Kim Ching & Co. and owned
rice mills at Saigon and in Siam.

49
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:22,300
He was Consul-General for Siam
in the Straits Settlements.

50
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:27,113
Song Ong Siang's 1923 history
records that his name was on the

51
00:03:27,193 --> 00:03:32,243
committee of promoters when the
dock company was started in 1863,

52
00:03:32,323 --> 00:03:37,373
that he was made a Justice of the
Peace in 1865, and that when he

53
00:03:37,453 --> 00:03:42,425
died in February 1892 he owned
the steamers Siam and Singapore.

54
00:03:42,425 --> 00:03:46,912
The same book notes that Ong
Kew Ho, a Malacca-born merchant,

55
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:51,853
was one of the few Chinese merchants
who believed in the future of

56
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:56,650
the enterprise and attended its
annual general meeting in 1871.

57
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:01,058
The company's first dock,
Victoria Dock, was opened by

58
00:04:01,138 --> 00:04:06,125
Governor Harry Saint George Ord
on the 17th of October 1868.

59
00:04:06,125 --> 00:04:08,250
It began badly.

60
00:04:08,250 --> 00:04:12,793
The Patent Slip and Dock Company,
which now faced a competitor,

61
00:04:12,873 --> 00:04:15,876
started a price war, and the Tanjong Pagar

62
00:04:15,956 --> 00:04:19,625
company had to cut its own
prices and made losses.

63
00:04:19,625 --> 00:04:25,090
Then the Suez Canal opened in 1869,
more steamships called at Singapore,

64
00:04:25,170 --> 00:04:29,175
and by the following year
the docks were profitable.

65
00:04:29,175 --> 00:04:34,404
The Patent company built a second
dock in 1870 and became the New

66
00:04:34,484 --> 00:04:39,877
Harbour Dock Company in 1875, and
in 1879 the Tanjong Pagar company

67
00:04:39,957 --> 00:04:45,675
opened Albert Dock, east of Victoria
Dock, to meet the growing demand.

68
00:04:45,675 --> 00:04:50,951
In 1871 a meeting was held at the
office of Boustead & Co. to form

69
00:04:51,031 --> 00:04:56,550
a company to build a railway from
the town to the company's wharves.

70
00:04:56,550 --> 00:04:57,950
The Hon.

71
00:04:57,950 --> 00:05:02,253
H. A. K. Whampoa was elected
one of its provisional directors

72
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:06,779
and was also a director of the
dock company, which was then one

73
00:05:06,859 --> 00:05:11,090
of only two limited-liability
companies in existence besides

74
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:15,625
the proposed railway company,
the other being the gas company.

75
00:05:15,625 --> 00:05:19,200
Meanwhile, the company
bought up smaller rivals.

76
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:22,911
In 1899 it merged with the
New Harbour Dock Company,

77
00:05:22,991 --> 00:05:27,504
and with that purchase it controlled
the whole of the shipping,

78
00:05:27,584 --> 00:05:32,250
dockyard and wharf business at
New Harbour except the P&O wharf.

79
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:35,650
Behind the docks was a large workforce.

80
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:39,736
Song Ong Siang's history
describes Chinese coolies carrying

81
00:05:39,816 --> 00:05:43,549
baskets of coal at the coal
wharves, and the company's

82
00:05:43,629 --> 00:05:47,725
own album has a photograph
titled "Coal coolies quarters."

83
00:05:47,725 --> 00:05:51,800
Much of the labour was
arranged through contractors.

84
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:55,884
Gan Eng Seng, who died in
1899, was labour contractor to

85
00:05:55,964 --> 00:06:00,642
the company and made a considerable
part of his fortune from it,

86
00:06:00,722 --> 00:06:04,881
and from December 1901 Tan
Kheam Hock held the same role,

87
00:06:04,961 --> 00:06:09,050
with a permanent labour force
numbering some thousands.

88
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:14,150
The company also kept its own
police force and fire brigade.

89
00:06:14,150 --> 00:06:18,542
By 1904 Singapore was the
seventh-largest port in the world,

90
00:06:18,622 --> 00:06:22,045
but it faced strong
competition from Hong Kong,

91
00:06:22,125 --> 00:06:26,145
and the company's wharf system
was under strain because

92
00:06:26,225 --> 00:06:30,250
no major improvements had
been carried out since 1885.

93
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:33,519
The Singapore board and
the London Consulting

94
00:06:33,599 --> 00:06:36,800
Committee disagreed
about capital spending.

95
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:40,855
In March 1904 the company
submitted a $12-million plan

96
00:06:40,935 --> 00:06:44,530
to upgrade and expand its
facilities, and it was

97
00:06:44,610 --> 00:06:47,746
rejected by its Europe-based shareholders,

98
00:06:47,826 --> 00:06:52,650
who feared the cost would "endanger
a dividend of 12 per cent."

99
00:06:52,650 --> 00:06:54,840
The company then asked the Straits

100
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:57,925
Settlements government
for financial support.

101
00:06:57,925 --> 00:07:02,300
The government decided instead
to take over the company.

102
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:06,732
The London committee, for its part,
had said it would not agree to

103
00:07:06,812 --> 00:07:11,325
cede control unless the dividend
was assured at the existing rate.

104
00:07:11,325 --> 00:07:15,158
The Tanjong Pagar Dock
Bill was introduced in the

105
00:07:15,238 --> 00:07:18,992
Legislative Council on
the 20th of January 1905,

106
00:07:19,072 --> 00:07:22,825
and its second reading
was on the 3rd of March.

107
00:07:22,825 --> 00:07:26,722
The Governor's stated ground,
as recorded by Song Ong Siang,

108
00:07:26,802 --> 00:07:30,766
was that the company had obtained
a practical monopoly of the

109
00:07:30,846 --> 00:07:34,677
port's shipping facilities,
and that it was undesirable for

110
00:07:34,757 --> 00:07:38,800
commerce that they should all
be under one company's control.

111
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:40,200
The Hon.

112
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:44,738
Mr Napier, supporting the bill,
said that public opinion outside

113
00:07:44,818 --> 00:07:49,725
those interested in the company was
practically unanimous in favour.

114
00:07:49,725 --> 00:07:52,899
He recalled that the first
chairman, Thomas Scott,

115
00:07:52,979 --> 00:07:57,455
had said the company's motto should
be "Live and let live," and argued

116
00:07:57,535 --> 00:08:01,881
that the company had since taken
over the Slipway, the Borneo Wharf,

117
00:08:01,961 --> 00:08:05,150
Jardine's Wharf and finally
the New Harbour Dock.

118
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:06,550
The Hon.

119
00:08:06,550 --> 00:08:11,208
Tan Jiak Kim added that the company
was not popular with the Chinese,

120
00:08:11,288 --> 00:08:14,367
and that for some years
Chinese shipping firms

121
00:08:14,447 --> 00:08:17,869
had been trying to avoid
sending their steamers and

122
00:08:17,949 --> 00:08:22,000
cargoes to Tanjong Pagar because
of its "enormous charges."

123
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,147
The bill passed on the 13th of April 1905,

124
00:08:25,227 --> 00:08:28,759
and on the 1st of July
the government took over

125
00:08:28,839 --> 00:08:32,450
the management as the
Tanjong Pagar Dock Board.

126
00:08:32,450 --> 00:08:37,340
The Straits Times of Saturday
the 1st of July 1905 printed

127
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:42,475
two notices signed by the
company's secretary, W. G. Niven.

128
00:08:42,475 --> 00:08:45,422
They said that the
company's undertaking had,

129
00:08:45,502 --> 00:08:49,795
under the Tanjong Pagar Dock
Ordinance 1905, become vested in the

130
00:08:49,875 --> 00:08:54,101
Government of the Straits Settlements
on that day, that it would

131
00:08:54,181 --> 00:08:57,868
be managed by the Tanjong
Pagar Dock Board, and that the

132
00:08:57,948 --> 00:09:01,850
Board would discharge the
company's debts and liabilities.

133
00:09:01,850 --> 00:09:05,328
A short item on the same
page recorded that the

134
00:09:05,408 --> 00:09:09,113
new "T.P.D.B." flag had
been hoisted for the first

135
00:09:09,193 --> 00:09:12,675
time on the office
flagstaff at Tanjong Pagar.

136
00:09:12,675 --> 00:09:16,144
The Governor had told the
Legislative Council the day

137
00:09:16,224 --> 00:09:19,962
before that the Board would
hold its first meeting on the

138
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:23,913
1st of July, and that a large
number of its members already

139
00:09:23,993 --> 00:09:27,475
had very long experience
of working the undertaking.

140
00:09:27,475 --> 00:09:32,050
The Board had nine appointed
members and two official members.

141
00:09:32,050 --> 00:09:35,877
One of the appointed members
was J. Rumney Nicholson,

142
00:09:35,957 --> 00:09:40,743
who had come to Singapore in 1902
as the company's general manager

143
00:09:40,823 --> 00:09:45,025
and later became chairman of
the Singapore Harbour Board.

144
00:09:45,025 --> 00:09:51,141
Compensation was settled by arbitration,
with Lord Saint Aldwyn as umpire,

145
00:09:51,221 --> 00:09:55,575
and the award was declared
on the 14th of July 1906.

146
00:09:55,575 --> 00:10:00,905
When the Under-Secretary of State
for the Colonies, Winston Churchill,

147
00:10:00,985 --> 00:10:05,774
answered questions about it in
the House of Commons on the 28th

148
00:10:05,854 --> 00:10:10,800
of November 1906, he said it
worked out at about $755 per share.

149
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:14,965
The National Library
Board's account gives $761,

150
00:10:15,045 --> 00:10:18,502
against the $600 that is recorded as the

151
00:10:18,582 --> 00:10:22,650
highest price the share
reached on the market.

152
00:10:22,650 --> 00:10:27,038
The questioner, Josiah Wedgwood,
put the other side of the case

153
00:10:27,118 --> 00:10:31,576
to the House: that the shares had
been quoted at about $240 when

154
00:10:31,656 --> 00:10:36,328
expropriation was decided on, that
the directors had mentioned $700

155
00:10:36,408 --> 00:10:40,725
to the Governor, and that with
a total capital of $3.7 million

156
00:10:40,805 --> 00:10:45,618
on which 12 per cent was paid, the
award came to about $28.5 million,

157
00:10:45,698 --> 00:10:49,377
or sixty-five years' purchase
of the annual dividend,

158
00:10:49,457 --> 00:10:54,350
against the fourteen years' purchase
the Governor had said was usual.

159
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:56,796
Churchill accepted that the figures

160
00:10:56,876 --> 00:10:59,475
were correct except in two respects.

161
00:10:59,475 --> 00:11:03,242
Twelve per cent, he said, was
the smallest dividend paid in

162
00:11:03,322 --> 00:11:07,350
recent years and dividends did
not accurately measure the value

163
00:11:07,430 --> 00:11:10,741
of the property, and the
government had taken over a

164
00:11:10,821 --> 00:11:15,125
considerable amount of land not
needed for the company's business.

165
00:11:15,125 --> 00:11:19,553
The Governor, he added, had told
the directors that 7 per cent

166
00:11:19,633 --> 00:11:23,987
was the ordinary rate of interest
in Singapore, which on that

167
00:11:24,067 --> 00:11:28,575
basis meant fourteen years'
purchase of the ordinary dividend.

168
00:11:28,575 --> 00:11:32,373
He also confirmed that the arbitration

169
00:11:32,453 --> 00:11:35,332
would cost well over £40,000,

170
00:11:35,412 --> 00:11:39,800
of which Lord Saint
Aldwyn received £5,750.

171
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:43,661
The Tanjong Pagar Dock
Board was reconstituted in

172
00:11:43,741 --> 00:11:48,003
1913 as a statutory body,
the Singapore Harbour Board,

173
00:11:48,083 --> 00:11:50,818
which that year opened King's Dock,

174
00:11:50,898 --> 00:11:56,125
the largest dock east of Suez,
and completed Empire Dock in 1917.

175
00:11:56,125 --> 00:11:59,196
The Board kept the company's
ship-repair business

176
00:11:59,276 --> 00:12:02,090
and controlled the whole
chain of repair work

177
00:12:02,170 --> 00:12:05,000
at Keppel Harbour for
the next five decades.

178
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,857
Through later changes of
name it became the Port

179
00:12:08,937 --> 00:12:11,399
of Singapore Authority in 1964,

180
00:12:11,479 --> 00:12:16,400
and eventually the Maritime and
Port Authority of Singapore.

181
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:18,768
Where it fits in the bigger story:

182
00:12:18,848 --> 00:12:24,825
Singapore's deep-water harbour is often
told as a story about governors and navies.

183
00:12:24,825 --> 00:12:28,661
This part of it began with an
agency house, a Chinese rice

184
00:12:28,741 --> 00:12:32,711
merchant and shareholders in
Singapore and London who wanted

185
00:12:32,791 --> 00:12:36,492
a dry dock, and it ended with
the government paying more

186
00:12:36,572 --> 00:12:40,204
than seven times face value
to own what they had built,

187
00:12:40,284 --> 00:12:44,200
at a price still being argued
over in London a year later.

188
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:46,600
The docks, the coaling wharves and the

189
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:49,225
people who worked them stayed in place.

190
00:12:49,225 --> 00:12:53,475
What changed in 1905
was who owned the port.
